Task 3

Why is software version information valuable to attackers?
A specific version number allows an attacker to search for known vulnerabilites, identify affected CVEs, locate exploit code, estimate the likelihood of successful exploitation.

Why are public exploit databases useful?
They can be used to identify known vulnerabilities, investigate affected versions and locate exploit code.

Does the existence of a public exploit guarantee successful exploitation?
Yes, once public exploit is found, the next step in the workflow is system compromising.

Why do attackers prefer reusing existing exploits instead of developing new ones?
There is high chance that the existing exploits can compromise the system without deploying new ones.

What additional information should be verified before attempting exploitation?
Before, we should gather informatiob, identify software versions and determine whether existing attacks can be reused.

