Task 8

What is a reverse shell?
A reverse shell is a connection initiated by the victim back to the attacker, giving the attacker a CLI.

Why must a listener be started before exploitation?
The listener must be started first because the victim connects to it. If nothing is waiting, the connection is lost.

Which side initiates the reverse shell connection?
The victim (Webmin container) initiates the connection back to us.

Why may the Python script display a timeout error?
The Python script times out because Webmin never sends an HTTP response, it's busy running the shell instead.

What advantages does an interactive shell provide?
A shell lets you run many commands interactively, explore the system, and pivot further.

How does this technique relate to the Metasploit exploit used earlier?
Metasploit automates listener setup, payload crafting, and delivery, in one run command.







